Subject: [M152] [mojo]Validate ipcz transport handles and sender trust

Original change's description:
> [mojo]: Validate ipcz transport handles and sender trust
>
> Sending handles between processes on Windows requires either the
> sender or the receiver to duplicate the handle into its peer process
> using DuplicateHandle(). Handles sent over ipcz are marked as kSender
> (meaning the sender still owns the handle and the receiver is
> responsible for duplicating it) or kRecipient (meaning the sender has
> already duplicated the handle into the receiver) to coordinate this
> handle transfer, since only one side should duplicate the handle.
> Moreover, duplicating a handle into another process is a powerful
> capability: if one side is more privileged, the convention is for the
> more privileged side to duplicate handles.
>
> When using a Mojo isolated connection, both sides are considered
> brokers. Optionally, one side can mark the other as untrusted using
> MOJO_SEND_INVITATION_FLAG_UNTRUSTED_PROCESS - in that case, the
> untrusted side should never be duplicating handles into its peer.
> However, two places in ipcz_driver::Transport failed to enforce safety
> invariants around handle duplication:
>
> 1. DecodeHandle() accepted HandleOwner::kRecipient handles from any
>    kBroker peer (unless the receiving endpoint itself was marked
>    elevated). Even though the untrusted sending broker could not
>    actually duplicate handles into the receiving process, it could
>    still guess numeric HANDLE values that happened to name pre-existing
>    handles in the receiving process's handle table.
> 2. Transport::Deserialize() allowed even untrusted peers to name a
>    transport's remote_process_.
>
> Changes:
> - DecodeHandle() no longer allows kRecipient handles from
>   explicitly-untrusted broker peers - this is encapsulated in
>   Transport::CanAcceptReceiverOwnedHandles().
> - Transport::Deserialize() only allows remote processes to be
>   specified if the sender is a non-broker and the receiver is a broker
>   (as in the case of mojo::DirectReceiver) or if the peer is not
>   marked as kUntrusted. This is encapsulated in
>   Transport::CanAcceptRemoteProcessFromPeer().
> - In the updater service, CreateServerEndpointOptions() previously
>   set MOJO_SEND_INVITATION_FLAG_UNTRUSTED_PROCESS for every caller of
>   every install. Now that DecodeHandle() rejects kRecipient handles
>   from untrusted peers, that declaration is made per caller, and only
>   by a system install (where callers cross a privilege boundary): a
>   new EndpointOptions::send_invitation_flags_callback lets
>   NamedMojoMessagePipeServer choose the invitation flags for each
>   connecting client, and the updater declares a caller untrusted if
>   its token is below High integrity. Such a caller cannot open the
>   SYSTEM server process and so never pre-duplicated legitimately. An
>   elevated administrator or SYSTEM caller is not declared untrusted
>   and keeps today's behavior. User installs, where both ends are
>   equally privileged, declare nothing.
> - To match the stricter checks in DecodeHandle(),
>   UpdateServiceProxyMojoImpl uses MOJO_SEND_INVITATION_FLAG_ELEVATED
>   when connecting to a system install on Windows. This keeps the more
>   privileged side doing the duplication even for callers capable of
>   opening the server process, and keeps such callers working should
>   the server's classification ever be tightened.
>
> Compatibility: clients built before this change keep working against
> a server built after it. A caller below High integrity cannot open the
> SYSTEM server process and therefore already sends kSender, which the
> server duplicates; a forged kRecipient handle from it is now refused.
> An elevated caller is not declared untrusted, so its pre-duplicated
> handles - including the link memory handle carried by the isolated
> connection handshake itself - are accepted as before. This matters
> because standalone installers bundle an older updater and run it
> elevated against whichever version is active.
>
